			<div class="content" id="post-content-2196970">
									

<p>The execution feels a little choppy:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;line-by-line</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;hide&nbsp;text</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<blockquote>
<p>Forty eight hours before broadcast, a countdown timer appears on the website chosen to host the webcast, and once it reaches zero the page automatically refreshes itself; in its place is a built-in video player.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This sentence is structured oddly and a little off for a scientific document. &quot;And once it reaches zero&quot; feels a little too much like narrative in this context, and this really could be broken up into two or three shorter sentences. Something along the lines of &quot;Forty-eight hours before broadcast of SCP-2160, a countdown timer appears on the chosen host website. Upon expiration of the timer, the host website's contents will be replaced with SCP-2160.&quot;</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Due to the coding on the website itself</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't get this part. This implies that there's preexisting coding on host websites for the skip, which doesn't seem like what you're describing.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>with the audio similar to other educational children's television shows</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&quot;Educational children's television shows&quot; is such a widely varying category of content that this description is completely meaningless. You'll want to be a little more specific here.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>No evidence that such a show was ever broadcast prior to the first known instance of SCP-2160.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Sentence fragment.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It was discovered that if an instance of SCP-2160 is downloaded or recorded from the broadcast site and then is played within a different program the visual portion of the instance functions as normal, revealing Sheriff Buddy to be a marionette dressed in a cowboy suit that resembles O5-6, and other puppets that re-enact events that do not coincide with the audio of the broadcast.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is another example of a long sentence that could be broken up for better flow. The way this is written also erroneously implies that Sheriff Buddy is also &quot;other puppets that reenact events&quot; etc.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>That, coupled with the memetic effect the footage has had on some,</p>
</blockquote>
<p>What? You didn't say anything about &quot;memetic effects&quot; in the description, and there's no explanation as to what exactly that &quot;memetic effect&quot; is. This part feels exceptionally shoehorned.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>A posthypnotic suggestion is placed into the mind of SCP-2599 to attempt to recreate the events of the broadcast, to see if her subconscious mind was capable of acting in a manner that her conscious mind could not.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is a huge, immersion-breaking stretch in terms of the limits of hypnotherapy.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Mobile Task Force Mu-4 was ordered to seize control of the broadcasting website the moment a transmission of SCP-2160 ended and broadcast a posthypnotic suggestion to make the viewers forget they had just watched SCP-2160.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>And here it is again. I get that the site relies heavily on amnestics and whatnot (though that's something I'd like to see a lot less of), but this is an after-the-fact handwaving away of a major containment breach without any real attempt at addressing the consequences on the narrative.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>00:02:22 - Sheriff Buddy: Secrets will always get discovered, no matter how hard you try to hide them kids, so be sure not to lie and keep things from others.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Oh good, another heavy handed &quot;evil Foundation&quot; moral.</p>
<hr /></div>
</div>
</div>
<p>There's three main issues, in addition to the writing-related problems above, that are making this not work for me.</p>
<ul>
<li>I feel like &quot;creepy kids show&quot; is kind of an overused trope. That doesn't necessarily make this bad because it's been done before, but the fact that this is a common thing speaks to the fact that this is a pretty easy and predictable source of &quot;creep&quot; to reach for. It gives the article's overall feel a predictable and hackneyed one as I read it, instead of the intended unsettling backdrop.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>I don't agree that this was a good use of crosslinks. The whole crosslinking issue is one that, like other critique memes, has gotten out of control and is now completely untethered from actual content. We have people on one side who say &quot;crosslink bad always no matter what&quot;, and it appears that here we have the other side of that argument, &quot;crosslinking stuff is good because crosslinks.&quot; There is no thematic connection at all with the skip that you're writing about and the things that you chose to reference. And I cannot tell you how tired I am of seeing Montauk show up, again and again, in various places on this site. It's just sort of, &quot;here's a few well-known articles to put in my article&quot;. I have no idea why the loop thing and why the girl who can't carry out commands are especially suited to be here. Thus, this is actually fairly good fodder for the anti-crosslink camp, as this comes across as coattail riding.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>&quot;Evil/Incompetent Foundation&quot; is not a trope I really care for at all. I'm more interested in the stories being told about the various anomalies encountered in the shared universe we inhabit than the organization that stewards them. I understand that's a personal thing, but the moralizing within the article about the whole &quot;keeping secrets&quot; thing was pretty sledgehammery, and I felt that could have been handled better. And I think that an organization so bad at doing its job of protecting consensus reality from the shadows that dozens of people literally show up at its sites because of an uncontained skip would have probably ended the world six times by now through its incompetence. Suspension of disbelief is always kind of tenuous around here, but the Foundation being poor at its mission always shatters it for me.</li>
</ul>
<p>All in all, this adds up to a solid downvote from me.</p>

