<p>Okay, to try to address your comments I'll use spoiler text, since it contains spoilers to my own headcanon on what is happening here:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">Here&nbsp;we&nbsp;go.&nbsp;Spoilers&nbsp;past&nbsp;this&nbsp;point.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>First, to start off, here is my personal headcanon for what is happening:</p>
<p>SCP-2160 wasn't originally the broadcast, it was a psionic who's powers only work while asleep. It stole the memories of the skips shown out of the minds of employees and used the broadcast as a way to try to alert people to the Foundation holding prisoners against their will without being too conspicuous. Once that failed, it started placing red herrings to keep the Foundation busy as it ramped up its efforts, until it reached a point where it gave away too much information and the O5 figured out who was doing it. The Cayce Protocol was enacted, and the documentation of the show was put into the place of the original SCP-2160's designation to minimize questions.</p>
<p>Now, to address the specific points:</p>
<p>1. I realize this is concerned a widely used trope when it comes to the initial imagery, but that was a conscious decision. The psionic in question wanted to use something that would get people to be curious while trying to not raise too many red flags with The Foundation itself too quickly. If you want to get the greatest amount of attention, you go with something you know works. I realize that puts me at a disadvantage from the start, but I was approaching this from what would make sense for the events taking place, not what was easiest for me. This is my first SCP for the site, I very much decided from the word go that easy wasn't going to be a word that went along with this.</p>
<p>&quot;Sheriff Buddy&quot;, specifically, was chosen because he's your &quot;friend&quot; who is saying one thing about keeping everyone safe while something else entirely is happening to you, which is how I imagine pretty much all humanoid skips feel about Foundation staff.</p>
<p>2. All the skips chosen were chosen for a very specific reason, namely the fact that to someone who doesn't know the specifics of said skip, they all appear to be trapped by the Foundation (and potentially experimented on) when one is denied total context of the situation. The psionic isn't omnipresent, and only has what it could steal from the memories of Foundation staff to work off of. Originally I had intended to make up events for each instance, but thedeadlymoose convinced me that there was enough meat with the idea that cross linking would work. I feel the story on its own if I had gone the other way still would have held up, and thus it's not &quot;riding coattails&quot;.</p>
<p>3. This is probably one of those &quot;agree to disagree&quot; points. I don't think the Foundation is anymore omnipresent than the skip they are dealing with, and as a result it takes them a significant amount of time to weed through the dozens of potential culprits they have capable of doing such a thing. If anything I feel I show a Foundation being cautious and showing restraint to keep themselves from blowing their own cover. The &quot;problems&quot; this skip was causing realistically wasn't anything that they couldn't resolve by giving an amnestic to anyone that bothered them (which is why it's not rated as a Keter, because the actual effort required by the Foundation to minimize damage was fairly mundane). In the end, the culprit was discovered before any &quot;real&quot; damage was done and the protocols to resolve the matter were put into place. I feel they handled the matter as best they could within their own protocols to not escalate things unnecessarily.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

