<p>First, to start off, here is my personal headcanon for what is happening:</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Okay, that's great and all, but almost none of that is really apparent in what you wrote, so I'm unsure how I'm to contextualize my critique in light of that information. Also, that doesn't really make a whole lot of sense, because it's unclear whether a) the original SCP-2160 was a humanoid already contained that's doing this, which given the off-site recording studio is nonsensical, or b) the original SCP-2160 was an uncontained something-or-other, in which case how did it know about all this stuff?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>The psionic in question wanted to use something that would get people to be curious while trying to not raise too many red flags with The Foundation itself too quickly. If you want to get the greatest amount of attention, you go with something you know works.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Soooo&#8230;this entity was intentionally using this kid's show format because it knew it was an overused trope in fiction? That's not really helping my reading of this.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>All the skips chosen were chosen for a very specific reason, namely the fact that to someone who doesn't know the specifics of said skip, they all appear to be trapped by the Foundation (and potentially experimented on) when one is denied total context of the situation.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Here's the thing about that. That makes perfect sense as an in-universe justification. However, that's not enough, because if you rely strictly on &quot;in-universe&quot; justification, we should be posting HR leave request forms and cafeteria menus because the Foundation would be reasonably expected to have those things from that perspective. We don't post those, though, because out-of-universe, no one wants to read those things. So you still have to deal with the out-of-universe, reader's perspective issue, which again is that there is no thematic cohesion to this stuff, beyond &quot;boy it's sad that these things are trapped at the Foundation,&quot; which is fairly thin IMO.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I feel the story on its own if I had gone the other way still would have held up, and thus it's not &quot;riding coattails&quot;.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Um, no. &quot;I could have written this without these references to well-known and popular articles and it wouldn't have been any different, but I left them in anyway&quot; is not exactly a defense here. If this article would have held up on its own without those references, why put them in there? Other than &quot;crosstesting is good because crosstests&quot; tautologies?</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I feel they handled the matter as best they could within their own protocols to not escalate things unnecessarily.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This point makes a little more sense after the authorial intent explanation above, although as previously stated there's still stuff that doesn't add up. Thing is, again, it was extremely difficult to arrive at that without the extra-article info. Because from my reading of it, based on what you wrote, it looks like a case of &quot;random dude somewhere knows about the Foundation for some reason&quot;.</p>
</div>
